\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Cross-environment evidence.}
The framework generates testable predictions beyond ScienceWorld. The MATH null ($p = 0.81$) validates the prediction that removing catastrophic failure eliminates routing signal; ALFWorld identifies information asymmetry as an alternative mechanism (\S\ref{sec:cross-env}). True quantitative DAF replication requires a second catastrophic-failure environment (e.g., NetHack~\citep{nethack-2020}, Crafter~\citep{crafter-2022}).

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and correlation structure.}
The $r = 0.97$ correlation is specific to catastrophic death ($P{=}100$); defining failure as any non-positive score yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$). As shown in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}, this correlation follows algebraically from death dominance when $P$ is constant; larger models with lower death rates would reduce DAF. This prediction assumes models above a minimum capability threshold; preliminary evidence (Qwen2.5-0.5B, $N{=}15$) suggests very weak models may exhibit low death rates not from avoidance ability but from inability to reach dangerous states.

\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
Death avoidance dominance (78.9\% of non-tie labels, DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$) reframes modality routing as a \emph{safety} problem, closer to a conservative default policy than to cost-quality optimization~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive, cogrouter-2026}. The underlying failure mechanism is state hallucination: the internal strategy predicts that an action succeeded when the environment state did not change, leading to cascading errors that trigger death. The deterministic strategy eliminates these compounding observation errors, while action-selection errors (16.3\% deterministic death rate) remain comparatively rare. The 86:9 label imbalance explains why always-deterministic is a strong baseline; 4B parameters for $+9$\,pp router improvement is consistent with the analytical framework being more efficient. This dominance is so thorough that even an oracle routing strategy with perfect per-task death risk knowledge cannot outperform always-deterministic (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold-routing}, Table~\ref{tab:threshold-routing}).

\paragraph{Framework applicability.}
The framework applies most directly to environments with sparse terminal rewards and catastrophic failure modes. Environments with graded failure costs would yield intermediate DAF values; stronger backbones with lower hallucination rates would reduce death-rate differentials and consequently lower DAF.

The most common failure mode for the internal strategy is state hallucination: the model predicts that an action succeeded (e.g., ``the water is now boiling'') when the environment state did not change, leading to cascading errors that trigger agent death. The deterministic strategy avoids this by receiving ground-truth observations, but still dies in 16.3\% of episodes, primarily from selecting inherently dangerous actions (e.g., eating toxic substances) rather than from state misestimation.

\section{Ablation Studies}
\label{app:ablations}

\paragraph{Capacity scaling.} We scale the implicit router from Qwen3-0.6B to Qwen3-4B and evaluate at both step and episode level (Table~\ref{tab:capacity}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Model & Step Bal.\ Acc.\ (\%) & Ep.\ Bal.\ Acc.\ (\%) \\
\midrule
Qwen3-0.6B & $56.4 \pm 3.5$ & $50.7 \pm 2.6$ \\
Qwen3-1.7B & $58.2 \pm 4.0$ & $43.1 \pm 1.0$ \\
Qwen3-4B & $59.2 \pm 3.5$ & $\mathbf{59.0 \pm 1.9}$ \\
\midrule
Always-det & 50.0 & 50.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Capacity scaling: step-level and episode-level balanced accuracy (4 seeds, identical configuration across all scales). Step-level accuracy is flat ($56$--$59\%$, within seed variance). At episode level, only 4B breaks the random baseline ($59.0\%$, $p < 0.005$).}
\label{tab:capacity}
\end{table}

Step-level balanced accuracy is flat across scales ($56$--$59\%$, within seed variance), indicating step-level discrimination is not capacity-sensitive. At episode level, only 4B breaks the random baseline ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at or below chance. The dominant explanation is that death avoidance constitutes a task-type-level binary signal that a step-level router cannot exploit efficiently.

\paragraph{Reasoning depth.} An ablation comparing chain-of-thought reasoning against direct-answer generation on 6 task types ($n{=}120$ episodes per condition, 6 tasks $\times$ 20 episodes per task) found no significant difference (Mann--Whitney $p > 0.26$, $|d| \leq 0.31$) and near-identical death rates (41.8\% vs.\ 42.5\%); reasoning depth does not meaningfully alter internal-mode outcomes.

\section{Threshold Routing Analysis}
\label{sec:threshold-routing}

Given per-task death risk knowledge, we test whether a simple threshold routing strategy can outperform always-deterministic. For each threshold $t \in [0, 1]$, we route to internal if the per-task internal death rate $\leq t$, otherwise to deterministic, and sweep all thresholds to find the optimal.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Strategy & Mean Score & Death Rate & $\Delta$ vs Det \\
\midrule
Always-det & $-9.47$ & $0.163$ & $0.00$ \\
Optimal threshold ($t{=}0$) & $-11.03$ & $0.155$ & $-1.56$ \\
Always-internal & $-39.07$ & $0.429$ & $-29.60$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Death-risk threshold routing analysis on 245 episodes. The optimal threshold routes only \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (the sole zero-death task) to internal, with all other tasks routed to deterministic. Bootstrap 95\% CI for the difference: $[-8.87, +5.92]$.}
\label{tab:threshold-routing}
\end{table}

Even with perfect per-task death risk knowledge, threshold routing cannot outperform always-deterministic. The sole zero-death task achieves higher scores under deterministic (+28.5) than internal (+18.9), leaving no room for a beneficial mixed strategy.

\end{document}

